{% extends '@CitadelAureum/core/docs/layout.html.twig' %}

{% block article %}
    <p>
        Access in Aureum is role-based. A role bundles module permissions
        (view and manage, per module), and an employee holds one or more roles.
        What someone sees in the menu is exactly what their roles allow.
    </p>

    <h2>View versus manage</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>View</strong> lets someone open a module and read it.
        <strong>Manage</strong> adds creating, editing, and deleting. Most
        front-desk roles need view on the directories and manage on the working
        modules they own; keep manage tight elsewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Common tasks</h2>
    <ol class="au-docs-steps">
        <li>Open <strong>Manager → Roles</strong> to see the hotel's roles.</li>
        <li>Create a role per job, not per person: "Front desk", "Housekeeping", "Duty manager".</li>
        <li>Assign roles when creating staff, or adjust an existing employee's roles from <strong>Manager → Staff</strong>.</li>
        <li>Use <strong>Manager → Modules</strong> to switch whole modules on or off for the hotel.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="au-docs-note">
        <i class="ph ph-warning"></i>
        <p>
            Managers can only grant permissions they hold themselves. You
            can't create a role more powerful than your own access.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
